## Choose checks by risk

For behavior changes, exercise the public entry point through real domain logic
to an observable outcome. Keep checks deterministic and derive expected results
independently when the contract needs an oracle. Isolate external effects at
adapters.

- Low risk (documentation, formatting, or isolated mechanical changes): inspect
  the diff and run relevant lightweight checks.
- Normal risk (behavior changes): add deterministic behavioral and regression
  checks for the changed contract.
- Elevated risk: add integration, recovery, migration, security, concurrency,
  or performance checks when the changed surface makes that failure mode
  relevant. State why each applicable check is covered or deferred.

Start with a failing test when the accepted behavior can be expressed as one.
Choose checks for evidence, not a blanket coverage target or ceremonial gate.

